[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: "Sparrow-2" text on screen over an office interior.
AUDIO: 'Everything we do here at Tavis is in service of bringing human computing to life. We\'re releasing Sparrow 2. It\'s our new conversational flow understanding model and it\'s the model that decides when the'

[00:13] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson, Staff Engineer at Tavus, speaking.
AUDIO: 'PAL should listen, when it should wait, when it should speak. Here\'s the thing about how humans do this. If I say to you, "So, the invoice number is," you already know that I\'m not done. And'

[00:29] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking.
AUDIO: 'you\'d wait for me 4 seconds, 5 seconds, 10 seconds, it doesn\'t matter. You would just decide to wait and you wouldn\'t have to think about it. But if I say, "That\'s everything. Thanks so much."'

[00:42] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking.
AUDIO: 'You\'d come back before the air settled. Same silence in both cases, completely different meaning. The silence didn\'t tell you that. The meaning of what I say and the way I said it did. It\'s kind of'

[00:54] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking, mentioning Sparrow-1.
AUDIO: 'like a dance and it runs underneath every conversation you\'ve ever had. And it\'s handled so smoothly that you\'ve never once thought about it. And Sparrow 1 pushed that approach much much further'

[01:07] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking about changing the approach.
AUDIO: 'than anyone else. It learned the phonic and the preodic shape of an end of turn. And it gave our AI a dramatically more natural conversational timing. So today we\'re changing the approach entirely.'

[01:21] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A UI showing "YOU" and "PAL" video feeds, with real-time metrics for "SPEAKING," "TURN TAKING - LAST 2 SECONDS," and "THE ROOM - LAST 2 SECONDS." "User still speaking 66%" and "Room noise 13%" are visible.
AUDIO: 'Sparrow 2 is still a real-time conversational model, but now it\'s added understanding. &gt;&gt; Hey, I got a call about my appointment. Uh, is this a is it still a good time?'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yes, your cleaning is still set for Monday at 400 p.m. with Drive Okafor. Let me know if you need anything else. &gt;&gt; All right. Um,'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you know what? Uh, do you have anything this Friday? &gt;&gt; I\'m still hearing some background noise. Could you try to mute or move a bit? We'

[01:59] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI showing "YOU" and "PAL" video feeds. "User still speaking 0%" and "Room noise 8%" are visible, indicating a pause.
AUDIO: 'do have a Friday opening at 9:20 a.m. with Drive Oka 4. Does that work for you? &gt;&gt; Yeah, actually Friday would work great. Thanks. &gt;&gt; But we didn\'t just tune Sparrow 1. We rebuilt it from the ground up around one'

[02:13] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: A flow diagram illustrating how audio is processed into various components like "speaker," "noise," "interrupt," "backchannel," leading to "conversational flow understanding."
AUDIO: 'idea. Don\'t reduce the audio down. Understand all of it. Everything the industry has been throwing away, the sentence you\'re midway through, the back channel, the interruption, the noise'

[02:27] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: The flow diagram showing audio processing into conversational flow understanding.
AUDIO: 'behind you, the mumble and the room itself. Spare 2 takes that all in and it makes decisions from that entire picture. And when you understand instead of just detecting,'

[02:40] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking, with an on-screen graphic comparing Sparrow-1's waiting time (around 3 seconds) to Sparrow-2's extended waiting capability (6-8 seconds).
AUDIO: 'the behavior changes. It feels much more natural. Sparrow one could maybe hold the floor for maybe 2 or 3 seconds when you weren\'t speaking. But Sparrow 2 can wait for six, seven, or 8 seconds'

[02:53] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking about Sparrow-2 feeling like it's listening.
AUDIO: 'because it\'s reading the sentence, not the syllable. When someone stops midthought to think and Sparrow 2 just waits, it feels amazing. It feels like it\'s listening to you.'

[03:07] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: A diagram showing Sparrow-2 processing "backchannel," "interrupt," "background noise," and "background speaker" inputs to inform the PAL.
AUDIO: 'Now, the part I\'m maybe most excited about. Sparrow 2 doesn\'t just make the timing decision. It works with the PAL. All of those labels, all of those scores about background noise, back channels,'

[03:21] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking about speech-to-text guessing.
AUDIO: 'interruptions, people speaking in the background, those flow into the rest of the system. So, the PAL can actually do something with them. Speech to text doesn\'t really know when someone\'s mumbling.'

[03:35] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A UI showing an audio waveform with "low clarity" detected, leading to the PAL saying, "Sorry — I didn't quite catch that."
AUDIO: 'It just guesses what they\'re saying. It hands over a clean, confident sentence of what it thought it heard to the LLM and then the conversations go sideways. But Sparit 2 knows. So instead of the'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'PAL confidently answering the wrong question, it can just say, "Hey, sorry. I didn\'t quite catch that. Can you say it again?" which sounds like a small thing, but an AI that admits it didn\'t hear you,'

[04:00] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson speaking about feeling like someone is listening.
AUDIO: 'that\'s the difference between feeling like you\'re talking to a computer and feeling like someone is listening to you. This breakthrough approach unlocks hundreds of new use cases anywhere that\'s loud or shared, kiosk, shop'

[04:13] [scene:use cases]
VISUAL: A graphic displaying various environments where Sparrow-2 can be applied, such as kiosks, retail, open offices, front desks, interviews, and cafés.
AUDIO: 'floors, those kinds of places, but also more intimate conversations where you really need to be heard. And the thing that you notice, the thing that everybody notices is that you stop'

[04:26] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Brian Johnson announcing Sparrow-2 is live.
AUDIO: 'thinking about the protocol. You stop thinking about your environment. You stop thinking about everything except the conversation and you just talk. I\'m really excited that Sparrow 2 is live now across all Tavis conversations. If'

[04:39] [scene:brand]
VISUAL: Tavus logo on a black background.
AUDIO: 'you\'re building conversational AI that has to keep up with real people in real rooms, go try it at tavis.io.'
